  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "A food web shows how plants and animals in an ecosystem are connected
    through feeding relationships. It explains who eats whom and how energy moves
    from one organism to another. Unlike a food chain, which shows a single path,
    a food web connects many different food chains.\nEnergy usually begins with producers,
    such as grass and other green plants. Plants make their own food using sunlight
    through photosynthesis and form the base of most food webs.\nAnimals that eat
    plants are called primary consumers or herbivores. For example, a grasshopper
    eats grass and receives energy from it. A frog may then eat the grasshopper,
    a snake may eat the frog, and an eagle may eat the snake. This creates an energy
    pathway:\n\nGrass \u2192 Grasshopper \u2192 Frog \u2192 Snake \u2192 Eagle\n\nThe
    arrows show the direction of energy transfer, from the organism being eaten to
    the organism that eats it.\nIn nature, animals usually have more than one food
    source. A frog may eat different insects, while a snake may eat frogs, mice,
    and other small animals. When several such food chains connect, they form a food
    web.\nAll organisms in a food web depend on one another. If the population of
    one organism changes, it can affect many others. For example, fewer frogs may
    lead to more grasshoppers and less food for snakes.\nFood webs help us understand
    how energy flows through ecosystems and how living organisms depend on each other
    to maintain ecological balance."
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: ecf77945acdc26144b2f70e021c9fd31, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 6468205213134445944, guid: ecf77945acdc26144b2f70e021c9fd31, type: 2}
